ORGO.reg({"types":{},"items":{"exam-q8-i":{"id":"exam-q8-i","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(i)","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Both groups are on wedges: both up. This is cis-1-ethyl-2-methylcyclohexane."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"The lecture's procedure, step 1. Two up groups on neighbouring carbons are one axial and one equatorial. Chair one: {{CH3}} axial, ethyl equatorial. The flip: {{CH3}} equatorial, ethyl axial. No wedges or dashes on a chair."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"Step 2. Each chair has one axial group. An axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol, an axial ethyl 1.79 (Table 5.3). The chair with the larger group, ethyl, equatorial is more stable, but only by about 0.05 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Step 3. The chairs are not identical and they are not mirror images of each other: one has an axial methyl and the other an axial ethyl. Stereoisomers that are not mirror images are diastereomers."}],"answer":"The chair with ethyl equatorial and methyl axial is slightly more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"cis-1,2 with different groups: larger group equatorial, chairs are diastereomers."}},"exam-q8-ii":{"id":"exam-q8-ii","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(ii)","subs":"1d:CH3,4u:CH(CH3)2","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1d:CH3,4u:CH(CH3)2]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"{{CH3}} on a dash (down), isopropyl on a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 4: trans-1-isopropyl-4-methylcyclohexane."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"Carbons 1 and 4 have axial bonds pointing in opposite directions. One group down on C1 and one up on C4 are therefore both axial in one chair and both equatorial in the other."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"The chair with both groups axial pays for both: 1.74 + 2.61 = 4.35 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The chair with both equatorial pays nothing. It is far more stable."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Different shapes, different energies, not mirror images: diastereomers."}],"answer":"The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"trans-1,4: both equatorial or both axial. Both equatorial wins."}},"exam-q8-iii":{"id":"exam-q8-iii","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(iii)","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Both methyls on wedges: cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. The lecture worked this exact molecule on 10/27."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"Chair one: the C1 methyl axial, the C2 methyl equatorial. The flip: C1 equatorial, C2 axial."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial methyl (1.74 kcal/mol) and one equatorial methyl. Same energy: K = 1. Neither is circled."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The chairs are not superimposable, but one is the mirror image of the other: the lecture calls them conformational enantiomers. They interconvert by the ring flip, which is why the molecule as a whole is achiral."}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. They are enantiomers (non-superimposable mirror images).","rule":"cis-1,2 with identical groups: equal energy, mirror-image chairs."}},"chair-both-s01":{"id":"chair-both-s01","type":"chair-both","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(iii)","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations, decide which is more stable, and say how the two chairs are related.\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Both methyls are on wedges: both up, so the molecule is cis."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"On neighbouring carbons, two up groups are one axial and one equatorial. The ring flip swaps them."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial methyl: equal energy, K = 1."},{"t":"Compare the shapes","h":"The two chairs are non-superimposable mirror images: conformational enantiomers."}],"answer":"cis; the two chairs are equal in energy; enantiomers","rule":"cis-1,2 with identical groups: equal energy, mirror-image chairs."},"role":"practice"}},"mols":{}});
